WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.679
A threat hunt should produce an outcome even when no compromise is found.

00:00:04.679 --> 00:00:09.963
Finding nothing in a scoped hunt can still be useful if the hypothesis was clear,

00:00:09.963 --> 00:00:13.485
the data sources were appropriate, and the limitations were documented.

00:00:13.485 --> 00:00:17.712
The outcome may be increased confidence, a rejected hypothesis, a better question,

00:00:17.712 --> 00:00:20.178
a telemetry gap, or a detection idea.

00:00:20.178 --> 00:00:23.348
Hunting is valuable because it improves the security program,

00:00:23.348 --> 00:00:26.166
not only because it occasionally finds an incident.

00:00:26.166 --> 00:00:28.632
One common output is new detection logic.

00:00:28.632 --> 00:00:32.859
If a hunt identifies a behavior pattern that can be monitored reliably,

00:00:32.859 --> 00:00:37.438
detection engineers can turn the query into a scheduled rule, correlation, or analytic.

00:00:37.438 --> 00:00:41.665
The detection should include clear logic, required data sources, expected false positives,

00:00:41.665 --> 00:00:43.426
severity guidance, and response steps.

00:00:43.426 --> 00:00:46.244
A hunt query may be exploratory and broad.

00:00:46.244 --> 00:00:49.414
A production detection needs repeatability, performance, ownership, and tuning.

00:00:49.414 --> 00:00:51.175
Another output is improved logging.

00:00:51.175 --> 00:00:55.050
A hunt may reveal that endpoint process events lack command-line detail,

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.164
DNS logs are missing client identifiers,

00:00:57.164 --> 00:01:00.334
cloud logs are retained for too short a period,

00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:02.799
or identity logs are not parsed consistently.

00:01:02.799 --> 00:01:03.856
Those findings matter.

00:01:03.856 --> 00:01:08.083
Better telemetry may be the most important result of the hunt because

00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:09.844
it makes future investigations stronger.

00:01:09.844 --> 00:01:11.606
Hunts can also tune alerts.

00:01:11.606 --> 00:01:14.071
If analysts repeatedly see benign administrative activity,

00:01:14.071 --> 00:01:17.594
the hunt may identify safe context for suppression or enrichment.

00:01:17.594 --> 00:01:19.707
Tuning should not simply hide noise.

00:01:19.707 --> 00:01:23.230
It should preserve the signal while reducing unnecessary analyst work.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:27.809
Sometimes the right answer is not suppression but better asset context, identity labeling,

00:01:27.809 --> 00:01:30.627
or an allow list with ownership and review.

00:01:30.627 --> 00:01:32.740
Response playbooks can improve as well.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:37.672
If a hunt shows that a suspicious behavior requires checking five related data sources,

00:01:37.672 --> 00:01:38.728
document that workflow.

00:01:38.728 --> 00:01:41.194
If escalation criteria were unclear, refine them.

00:01:41.194 --> 00:01:43.307
If incident responders need specific fields,

00:01:43.307 --> 00:01:46.830
add those fields to the detection output or triage notes.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.648
Hunting and incident response should feed each other.

00:01:49.648 --> 00:01:51.761
Threat intelligence feedback is another outcome.

00:01:51.761 --> 00:01:56.341
A hunt may show whether a reported behavior is relevant to the environment,

00:01:56.341 --> 00:02:00.215
which assets would be exposed, or which controls already provide coverage.

00:02:00.215 --> 00:02:03.385
It may also produce internal intelligence about normal behavior,

00:02:03.385 --> 00:02:06.203
such as common administrative tools, expected service-account patterns,

00:02:06.203 --> 00:02:07.612
and typical cloud actions.

00:02:07.612 --> 00:02:11.487
For beginner hunters, the key idea is simple: close the loop.

00:02:11.487 --> 00:02:15.009
Do not let the hunt end at the last query.

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:17.827
Document the result, preserve useful evidence, identify gaps,

00:02:17.827 --> 00:02:21.702
recommend detection or logging changes, and share what the team learned.

00:02:21.702 --> 00:02:24.168
That is how hunting becomes continuous improvement.

00:02:24.168 --> 00:02:25.577
Follow-through should have ownership.

00:02:25.577 --> 00:02:30.156
If the hunt recommends a detection, someone should own writing and testing it.

00:02:30.156 --> 00:02:34.031
If it recommends better logging, someone should own the platform change.

00:02:34.031 --> 00:02:38.610
If it identifies an asset context gap, someone should own the data update.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.780
Without ownership, hunt outcomes become notes instead of improvements.
